backend/data/optimus/learner/states/sweep_meta*.json

# EDGAR 8-K item-code TAPE (87 MB, 293,619 rows). A DERIVED tape, rebuilt by
# `scripts/edgar_8k_items.py` from filings we already have; the RECEIPT beside
# it is committed. GitHub warns above 50 MB and rejects above 100 MB, and this
# repo is public. Data manifest: docs/DATA_MANIFEST.md.
backend/data/optimus/edgar_8k/*.jsonl

# COMPANYWORLD v1 working artefacts (2026-09-06). The PRODUCT --
# `backend/data/optimus/graph/companyworld_v1.parquet`, 2,020 edges, 18 KB -- IS
# committed, and so is every receipt. These two directories are not:
#   companyworld_work/   31 MB -- the per-document extraction records the run
#                        resumes from, plus the cached 10-K bodies. Re-derivable
#                        by `scripts/companyworld_extract.py`, and re-buying them
#                        costs about $2.12.
#   companyworld_inputs/ 57 MB -- copies of two CRSP/Compustat link registries
#                        that already live under backend/data/. A second copy of
#                        a source is a second source.
# This repo is public and GitHub rejects blobs above 100 MB.
# Data manifest: docs/DATA_MANIFEST.md.
backend/data/optimus/graph/companyworld_work/
backend/data/optimus/graph/companyworld_inputs/

# I1 — SEC Insider Transactions bulk sets (2026-09-07). The RAW quarterly ZIPs
# (~13 MB x 82 = ~1.0 GB) and the parsed per-quarter parquet are SUBSTRATE,
# re-downloadable for free from sec.gov by `scripts/sec_insider_bulk_load.py`.
# The committed evidence is the per-quarter receipts, the cursor and the
# coverage-by-year receipt beside them. `*.parquet` is already ignored above.
# Data manifest: docs/DATA_MANIFEST.md.
backend/data/optimus/sec_insider/raw/
backend/data/optimus/sec_insider/_load.log
backend/data/optimus/sec_insider/_load.pid

# ── Lane L (2026-09-07): scraped RAW BODIES are DATA, not receipts ──────────
# `backend/data/scrape/<source>/` holds gzipped bodies straight off the wire,
# an index, cursors, a log and a pid. It is regenerable by re-running the
# puller and it grows without bound (GDELT's gkg stream alone is ~4.5 MB per
# 15-minute stamp, ~430 MB/day), so it does not belong in git.
#
# So `bodies/` is ignored and the PROVENANCE is not: `index.jsonl`, `cursor.json`,
# `pull.log` and `coverage.json` are small, are the evidence that a pull happened
# and got where it says it got, and are what a resume reads. Ignoring those too
# would leave the next session unable to tell an unfetched window from an
# unrecorded one -- the same failure the 83.6% pull had.
#
# `pull.pid` is ignored: a pid is true of one machine for one run, and a
# committed one would read as a live process on somebody else's box.
#
# `backend/data/optimus/free_inference_2026-09-07/` is RECEIPTS and is tracked.
backend/data/scrape/*/bodies/
backend/data/scrape/*/pull.pid
.gdelt_bg.out
.nim_bg.out

# ── Lane R7 (2026-09-08): trained encoder WEIGHTS are DATA, not receipts ────
# `backend/data/optimus/r7_news_representation/encoder_*.pt` are four self-
# supervised transformer checkpoints (70 MB total). They are regenerable in
# 11-109 minutes each from a corpus that lives in the OTHER repo, they carry no
# evidence a reader needs, and a public repo is not a model registry.
#
# Everything else in that directory stays TRACKED and is small: the R7_*.json
# receipts (every headline number in
# docs/BUILD_2026-09-08_R7_NEWS_REPRESENTATION.md), the per-arm monthly CSVs
# (83 rows each -- the IC, the book, the cost and the holdings of every test
# month), and the per-slice vocabularies (which are the ONLY way to tell later
# whether a PIT encoder's vocabulary was fitted on future text; ignoring those
# would delete the evidence for the quietest leak this lane guards against).
# The .parquet intermediates are already covered by the repo-wide `*.parquet`.
backend/data/optimus/r7_news_representation/encoder_*.pt

# night factory: the evidence files are DATA (tens of MB, regenerated by the
# queue); the receipts (*.json) and LEADERBOARD.md stay tracked
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/G1_evaluations.jsonl
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/N1_configs*.jsonl
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/C1_counterfactual_news.jsonl
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/*.log
# ...and for every evolutionary job after G1. The G1 line names G1, so G3's
# evaluation log was NOT ignored and a `git add -A` would have committed it.
# A rule that names one job is a rule that will be wrong at the next job.
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/*_evaluations.jsonl
# smoke receipts are test artefacts of a 4-year tape or a 25-symbol pull; they
# carry job-shaped filenames and would otherwise read as results on the board
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/*_smoke.json
# 2026-09-10: the smoke rule missed the smoke JSONL and the smoke search state.
# G3's per-evaluation log is appended to, so a smoke run's rows land in the same
# family of file as a real run's; committing one puts test rows next to results.
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/*_smoke.jsonl
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/*_smoke/
backend/data/optimus/night_factory_*/G3_checkpoint_run*.json

# 2026-09-10 runtime state and derived caches, not results:
# - the llama-server ownership note is a PID and a timestamp for THIS machine
# - the frozen-encoder cache is 120 MB regenerated from the panel in minutes
backend/data/optimus/llama_server_owner.json
backend/data/optimus/text_return_panel/emb_*/

# The desktop window's browser profile (localStorage). Per-machine state that exists
# so the guide and the tour are dismissed ONCE; nothing in it is shareable.
backend/data/optimus/webview_profile/

# 2026-09-11 (E6): the evidence ledger rotated into MONTHLY files. The single
# `evidence_memory.jsonl` was 65.16 MB over 102,029 rows and grew every night;
# GitHub rejects a blob at 100. The LIVE month is the one being appended to, so
# it is ignored -- committing it would recreate the same growing blob under a
# new name. A month that has CLOSED is sealed and added once, deliberately:
#     git add -f backend/data/optimus/learner/evidence_memory_2026-09.jsonl
# (a rule here never applies to an already-tracked file, so a sealed month stays
# tracked for good). The split is by each row's own `utc`, never by mtime --
# see `scripts/evidence_memory_rotate.py`.
backend/data/optimus/learner/evidence_memory_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].jsonl
backend/data/optimus/learner/evidence_memory_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].jsonl.tmp

# 2026-09-12 (T6): the LLM SPEND ledger rotated the same way, for the same
# reason. `llm_calls.jsonl` was 62.25 MB over 92,737 rows and every LLM call
# appends to it; GitHub had already WARNED on a push at 50 MB and refuses at
# 100. Compaction is REFUSED -- a spend ledger that cannot be re-derived per
# call is not a ledger -- so the LIVE month is ignored and a CLOSED month is
# sealed once, deliberately:
#     git add -f backend/data/optimus/llm_calls_2026-09.jsonl
# `test_llm_calls_rotation.py` fails if a closed month on disk is untracked,
# and the same guard now covers both families. The split is by each row's own
# `ts`, never by mtime -- see `scripts/llm_calls_rotate.py`.
backend/data/optimus/llm_calls_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].jsonl
backend/data/optimus/llm_calls_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].jsonl.tmp
# torn lines copied out of the monolith by the rotation: an address book, not a
# ledger. Local evidence of local damage.
backend/data/optimus/llm_calls_unstamped.jsonl

# 2026-09-11 (O1, the thin launcher). `dist/` and `build/` were already ignored;
# the staging paths are not covered by those rules and a 37 MB bundle plus its
# analysis cache would otherwise be swept in by a `git add -A`. The staging path
# exists because PyInstaller's COLLECT wipes its target first, so a rebuild
# while the app is open dies with a permission error forty lines deep.
dist_next/
build_next/

# What the launcher and the shell WROTE on this machine, this launch: which
# checkout was found, whether the pull was skipped, the port, the model server's
# PID. Per-machine runtime state, useful for ten seconds, never shared.
backend/data/optimus/launch_receipt.json
backend/data/optimus/aegis_desktop_report.json

